  "simplified_explanation": "This article introduces Tim Costello, a well-known Australian who has dedicated his life to fighting poverty. When he was a teenager in school (year 9), he asked his teacher whether poverty was just a natural part of life that couldn't be changed — like the weather or the ocean tides. He came to believe the answer was no — poverty isn't something that just happens naturally. Instead, it's caused by the decisions and policies that governments and societies make. This realization shaped the rest of his life.\n\nThe article describes him sitting on a beach near his home in Frankston, Australia, at age 69, looking back on a career spent trying to help poor people both in Australia and around the world. He has done many impressive things: he was the mayor of a place called St Kilda, he wrote books, he visited war zones and disaster areas, and he was even recognized as a 'national living treasure' — meaning Australians consider him an important and admired person.\n\nHe is often mixed up with his brother, Peter Costello, who was a powerful politician (the treasurer, which is like the person in charge of the country's money) in Australia's Liberal Party. People joke about telling the two apart by calling one 'the Liberal one' (the politician brother) and 'the churchy one' (Tim, who is a Baptist minister — a type of Christian religious leader). Tim is most famous for running World Vision, a large charity that helps people in poverty around the world, for 13 years."
